# Proving a lowering change is safe

There is **no CI on `lib/**` or `ext/**`** (see `local-ci-only.mdc` — GitHub Actions is off by
choice). The single workflow that exists carries a `paths:` filter that excludes them. So:

> **`gh pr view` reporting `MERGEABLE` / `CLEAN` means "no checks are configured", not "checks
> passed."** Never cite it as evidence. Say which gate you ran locally.

Because nothing catches you automatically, the burden is on the change author to run the right gate
and to report honestly what was and was not covered.

## The gate that catches silent wrong output

The compliance suite asserts against **recorded** expectations, so it only catches what someone
already thought to record. Differential execution asserts against **Zend**, which is what catches
code that runs to completion and prints the wrong thing:

```bash
script/differential-sweep.sh              # VM backend
script/differential-sweep.sh --aot        # AOT backend — slower, but the path with the least coverage
script/differential-sweep.sh --dir DIR    # your own programs
```

Exit status is the mismatch count. **Run this for any change to argument handling, call lowering,
operand/slot resolution, or CFG shape.** It found 24 silent-wrong-output defects that the compliance
suite did not (#23354), and would have caught the AOT defects #23471/#23472.

New cases must be **deterministic** — no clocks, no randomness, no network, no object ids, no hash
order. A case that varies between runs makes the sweep useless as a gate.

## Comparing compliance suites: names, never counts

**Neither suite is green on master** (~407 `VMTest`, ~472 `JITTest` pre-existing failures). A raw
failure count therefore proves nothing. Compare the **set difference of failing case names**,
branch vs master, restricted to cases that completed on both sides.

Run both sides. "It fails on my branch" is not a finding until you know whether it fails on master.

## Making the suites runnable

Serial `VMTest` is ~4 h and `JITTest` does not finish in 40 min, which is why they do not get run.
Shard them:

- split the case list N ways (24 is a good default) and run N containers with `--filter`;
- use `--teamcity` so per-test `testStarted`/`testFailed`/`testFinished` are emitted **as they run** —
  default output prints failures only at the end, so a stalled shard yields nothing;
- cap each shard with `timeout`, and compare only cases that completed on both sides.

Two traps that silently produce "everything passed":

- `--filter` takes a PHP regex; an unescaped `/` inside it closes the delimiter early, matches
  nothing, and prints **"No tests executed!" with exit code 0**. Escape as `\/`.
- `--list-tests` **ignores** `--filter`, so it cannot be used to verify a shard's selection.

## Flaky tests are quarantined, not tolerated

These flip between runs and corrupt regression comparisons — one appeared on the *fixed* list in one
comparison and the *regressed* list in the next:

- `stdlib/hrtime*` — `hrtime()` is not monotonic here (#23420)
- `stdlib/proc_get_status_basic`
- `language/interface_abstract_static_call`, `types/dnf_return_type_error` — order-dependent
- `stdlib/password_needs_rehash` — does not terminate under the interpreted VM

**Before reporting any case as a regression, run it individually on both master and your branch,
several times.** Every "regression" investigated this way so far turned out to fail on master too.
Entry to this list is a bug to fix, not a place to park things.

## Reporting

State the gate you ran, the comparison basis, and what you did **not** cover. "Differential sweep
green (43/43 VM); compliance not run" is useful. "Tests pass" is not.
